---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { APP_NAME } from '../lib/app-icon.mjs';

// /app — how to put the journal on a home screen.
//
// A SMALL STATIC PAGE, AND STATIC IS THE POINT. It could sniff the user agent
// and show one set of steps; it does not. A reader on a desktop sending the
// link to their own phone, a reader whose browser lies about itself, and a
// reader following a friend's instructions all need to see the other platform's
// steps, and the page is four lines long either way.
//
// NO INSTALL BUTTON. `beforeinstallprompt` would give Android a one-press
// install and would give iOS — where most of this journal's phone readers are —
// nothing at all, at the cost of a script on a page whose entire content is
// four sentences. The steps work everywhere and go stale nowhere.
---

<Base
  title="Add the app"
  description={`How to add ${APP_NAME} to a phone's home screen.`}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="kicker kicker--accent kicker--lead">The app</p>
      <h1>On your home screen</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="app-mark">
      <img src="/favicon.svg" width="96" height="96" alt={`The ${APP_NAME} app icon`} />
      <p class="app-mark-line">{APP_NAME} on your home screen.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        There is nothing to download and no store to visit. Your browser makes the journal
        into an app itself: an icon that opens straight to the current issue, without the
        address bar.
      </p>

      <h2>iPhone and iPad</h2>
      <p>
        Open the journal in <strong>Safari</strong> — this does not work from Chrome or from a
        link opened inside another app. Press <strong>Share</strong>, the square with the arrow
        out of it, then <strong>Add to Home Screen</strong>, then <strong>Add</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Android</h2>
      <p>
        Open the journal in <strong>Chrome</strong>. Press the <strong>⋮</strong> menu, then
        <strong>Add to Home screen</strong> or <strong>Install app</strong> — Chrome offers
        whichever it offers — then confirm.
      </p>

      <h2>Desktop</h2>
      <p>
        In Chrome or Edge, an install icon appears at the right-hand end of the address bar.
        Safari on macOS installs from <strong>File → Add to Dock</strong>. Firefox does not
        install web apps, and the journal reads perfectly well as a page.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get, and what we get</h2>
      <p>
        You get the journal, and only the journal. Adding it to a home screen installs no
        software beyond the page you are already reading, asks for no permissions, sends no
        notifications, and can be removed by deleting the icon. It does not tell us that you
        installed it. There is nothing here that a bookmark does not do; the icon is simply a
        faster door.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .app-mark {
    text-align: center;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 3rem;
  }

  /* The same rounding as the footer mini, at the size a home screen shows it —
     the reader is looking at the thing they are about to install. */
  .app-mark img {
    border-radius: 18px;
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .app-mark-line {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
  }

  /* The platform headings are signposts in a list of instructions, not sections
     of an argument, so they take the site's small-caps label voice rather than
     the display serif a piece's headings use. */
  .prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
  }
</style>
